<% content_for :title, "Files — Tinkerex" %>

<div class="page-head">
  <h1>Files</h1>
  <%# List/grid toggle. files_query_base rides along so the choice keeps whatever the user is
      looking at (the folder, and from Task 6 the type filter) instead of dumping them at the
      root; the choice itself is remembered in a cookie (FilesController#resolve_view), so it
      sticks across navigation, and the sort is remembered the same way rather than being pinned
      into these two links. %>
  <div class="view-toggle" role="group" aria-label="View">
    <%= link_to "Grid", files_path(files_query_base.merge(view: "grid")),
                class: "toggle#{' active' if @view == 'grid'}" %>
    <%= link_to "List", files_path(files_query_base.merge(view: "list")),
                class: "toggle#{' active' if @view == 'list'}" %>
  </div>
</div>

<%#
  Breadcrumb: root -> current folder, each segment a link. Root always shows; @crumbs is [] at
  the root, so this collapses to just "Files" there. Segments walk down the path the controller
  built by climbing parent_node_id (already user-scoped), so nothing here re-derives the tree or
  trusts params[:folder] a second time. aria-current marks the leaf for assistive tech.
%>
<% content_for :crumbs do %>
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Folder path">
    <%= link_to "Files", files_path %>
    <% @crumbs.each_with_index do |crumb, i| %>
      <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <% if i == @crumbs.length - 1 %>
        <%= link_to crumb.name, files_path(folder: crumb.node_id), "aria-current": "page" %>
      <% else %>
        <%= link_to crumb.name, files_path(folder: crumb.node_id) %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
    <% if @q %>
      <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Search “<%= @q %>”</span>
    <% end %>
  </nav>
<% end %>

<%= render "ext_filter" %>

<%# HIDDEN-FILE TOGGLE. Dot-prefixed entries are noise in a maker's library — 78 of them on the
    deployed share box, mostly `.gitignore`, `.github` and `.clang-format` sitting between the
    models — but hiding them with no way back is how a file becomes unreachable, so this is a
    toggle rather than a silent filter.

    THE STATE LIVES IN A COOKIE (FilesController#resolve_hidden), not in files_query_base, for the
    same reason `view` and `sort` do: the choice has to survive clicking into a folder, and folder
    links carry only `folder:`. The link therefore sends the value it wants rather than a bare
    flag — `hidden=0` is a real instruction to hide, not an absent parameter.

    `.git` STAYS HIDDEN EITHER WAY and that is not an oversight — Node#visible_entries explains why
    the marker is a different rule from the clutter. %>
<p class="hidden-toggle muted">
  <% if @show_hidden %>
    <%= link_to "Hide dot-files", files_path(files_query_base.merge(hidden: "0")) %>
  <% else %>
    <%= link_to "Show dot-files", files_path(files_query_base.merge(hidden: "1")) %>
  <% end %>
</p>

<% if @truncated %>
  <p class="truncated">
    Showing the first <%= number_with_delimiter(@nodes.size) %> of
    <%= number_with_delimiter(@match_count) %> matching files — narrow the filter to see the rest.
  </p>
<% end %>

<%#
  FOLDER PAGING. Rendered only when there IS more than one page, so an ordinary folder looks
  exactly as it always did — this bounds huge directories, it is not a new widget on every page.

  The links carry files_query_base (the folder) PLUS sort/dir. sort and dir are deliberately
  omitted from in-page links elsewhere because they are cookie-persisted, but a page link is the
  one place they must be explicit: "page 3" only means anything under the ordering that produced
  it, so a link that dropped them could hand the next request a different sort and therefore a
  different page 3.
%>
<% if @paged && @pages > 1 %>
  <nav class="pager" aria-label="Folder pages">
    <% if @page > 1 %>
      <%= link_to "‹ Previous",
                  files_path(**files_query_base, sort: @sort, dir: @dir, page: @page - 1),
                  rel: "prev", class: "pager-prev" %>
    <% end %>
    <span class="pager-status">
      Page <%= number_with_delimiter(@page) %> of <%= number_with_delimiter(@pages) %>
      (<%= number_with_delimiter(@child_count) %> items)
    </span>
    <% if @page < @pages %>
      <%= link_to "Next ›",
                  files_path(**files_query_base, sort: @sort, dir: @dir, page: @page + 1),
                  rel: "next", class: "pager-next" %>
    <% end %>
  </nav>
<% end %>

<%#
  UPLOAD — when a project is CURRENT, the single upload button defaults into it (its Projects/<slug>
  folder + attach), reusing the EXISTING project upload/attach control (spec 2026-07-23) — no new
  controller, no new JS. A small link overrides to a plain library-root upload (?upload=library)
  without clearing the current project. When no project is current, or the override is active, the
  plain two-flow upload renders exactly as before:

  ONE BUTTON, TWO FLOWS -- and this branch is why `port_writable` exists. It does NOT ask "can
  this deployment accept uploads": both of these upload. It asks WHICH DOOR the bytes go in, and
  the answer is the deployment's, not the user's. Rendering the wrong form is not a cosmetic bug:
  the JS uploader would drive begin_upload, which FilesController answers 501 on a share/s3
  library, so the button would simply fail.
%>
<% if current_project && params[:upload] != "library" %>
  <div class="upload-to-project">
    <span class="muted">Uploading to project: <strong><%= current_project.name %></strong></span>
    <%= render "projects/upload_control",
               upload_url: project_uploads_path(current_project),
               attach_url: project_attachments_path(current_project),
               redirect_url: files_path(folder: @current_project_folder_node_id),
               parent_node_id: @current_project_folder_node_id %>
    <%= link_to "Upload to library root instead",
                files_path(files_query_base.merge(upload: "library")), class: "muted upload-alt" %>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <% if capabilities.port_writable? %>
    <%# Share/S3: no chunk store to presign into, so the bytes come up in one multipart POST. %>
    <%= form_with url: files_path, method: :post, class: "uploader",
                  data: { turbo: false }, html: { multipart: true } do |f| %>
      <label class="upload-btn">
        <%= f.file_field :file, onchange: "this.form.requestSubmit()", hidden: true %>
        <span>Upload a file</span>
      </label>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <%# Chunk: the browser hashes, we presign the missing chunks, the browser PUTs them (§15). %>
    <section class="uploader"
             data-controller="uploader"
             data-uploader-begin-url-value="<%= begin_upload_files_path %>"
             data-uploader-finalize-url-value="<%= finalize_upload_files_path %>">
      <label class="upload-btn">
        <input type="file" data-uploader-target="input" data-action="uploader#upload" hidden>
        <span>Upload a file</span>
      </label>
      <span class="upload-status" data-uploader-target="status"></span>
    </section>
  <% end %>
  <% if current_project %>
    <%= link_to "Upload to project “#{current_project.name}” instead",
                files_path(files_query_base), class: "muted upload-alt" %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<%#
  RESCAN, offered ONLY on a scannable (share/s3) library: the catalog there is periodically
  polled from the store (spec decision 5), so a user who just edited files on the NAS can catch
  the index up now instead of waiting for the 15-minute recurring Library::ScanJob. Chunk mode's
  catalog is the sync engine's changelog -- nothing to poll -- so the button is hidden AND
  FilesController#rescan refuses the POST (require_scannable!). `capabilities.scannable?` RAISES
  if misspelled (the concern's helper, not an ivar with &.), so a typo fails at render in a test
  rather than silently vanishing. `folder:` rides along so the redirect lands back in this folder.
%>
<% if capabilities.scannable? %>
  <%= button_to "Rescan library", rescan_files_path(files_query_base),
                class: "linkbtn", data: { turbo: false } %>
<% end %>

<% if @nodes.empty? %>
  <%# Three emptinesses. "No files yet" is advice for a new library; said to someone who searched
      or filtered, it reads as "your library is empty" and is a lie. %>
  <% if @q && @exts.any? %>
    <p class="empty muted">No files match “<%= @q %>” among <%= @exts.map { |e| ".#{e}" }.to_sentence %>.</p>
  <% elsif @q %>
    <p class="empty muted">No files match “<%= @q %>”.</p>
  <% elsif @flat %>
    <p class="empty muted">No files match <%= @exts.map { |e| ".#{e}" }.to_sentence %>.</p>
  <% else %>
    <p class="empty muted">No files yet. Upload one to get started.</p>
  <% end %>
<% elsif @view == "grid" %>
  <%#
    The grid has no column headers to click, so it gets the same sort as a pair of selects. GET,
    so the result is a plain shareable URL exactly like the header links produce. `onchange`
    submits without JS of our own (an inline handler on a native form, not a controller) — the
    same shape models/index.html.erb's toolbar already uses.
  %>
  <form method="get" action="<%= files_path %>" class="sort-form">
    <% files_query_base.each do |k, v| %>
      <input type="hidden" name="<%= k %>" value="<%= v %>">
    <% end %>
    <label class="muted" for="sort-key">Sort</label>
    <select name="sort" id="sort-key" onchange="this.form.requestSubmit()">
      <% { "name" => "Name", "type" => "Type", "modified" => "Modified" }.each do |value, label| %>
        <option value="<%= value %>" <%= "selected" if @sort == value %>><%= label %></option>
      <% end %>
    </select>
    <select name="dir" aria-label="Sort direction" onchange="this.form.requestSubmit()">
      <option value="asc"  <%= "selected" if @dir == "asc" %>>Ascending</option>
      <option value="desc" <%= "selected" if @dir == "desc" %>>Descending</option>
    </select>
  </form>
  <%= render "grid" %>
<% else %>
  <%= render "list" %>
<% end %>
